to-vitalik-buterin-source : List Word
to-vitalik-buterin-source = lines-of (cons "# Vitalik Buterin"
  (cons ""
  (cons "You have spent a decade on a question most of economics treats as a footnote: how a community pays for what everyone uses and nobody can own. Quadratic funding, retroactive public goods funding and d/acc are three answers to it, each more exact than the field's habit of handing the question to states or to charity. Everything here that builds on your ideas credits them to you in full. Each claim below about Avik Jain's work links to the block of his paper, *hyperactivity*, Part I, that states it."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Your work, read as exact"
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Quadratic funding.** In *A Flexible Design for Funding Public Goods* (with Zoë Hitzig and Glen Weyl, *Management Science*, 2019), a project receives the square of the sum of the square roots of its contributions, and under the paper's assumptions this reaches the efficient level of funding that markets and one-dollar-one-vote both miss. That result belongs in every economics curriculum, and it mostly is not there."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Retroactive public goods funding.** Your 2021 post with the Optimism team rests on one observation: it is easier to agree on what was useful than on what will be useful. Funding turns from forecasting into recognition. The open problem you named is measurement: how credit is traced through everything that depended on the work."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**d/acc.** In *My techno-optimism* (November 2023) you argued for accelerating technologies that favor defense and decentralize power. Your May 2026 post on AI-assisted formal verification carries this into code, naming STARKs, ZK-EVMs, consensus protocols and post-quantum cryptography as targets, and naming the limit honestly: a proof can miss the assumptions it never modeled."
  (cons ""
  (cons "These share one structure: each replaces a judgment that rested on trust in an authority with one that can be computed, checked or recorded."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What hyperactivity is"
  (cons ""
  (cons "In Avik's words, hyperactivity is “a language and a computational model of reality i.e. wolfram's ruliad executing on silicon”. Its core is the CCHM cubical calculus with clock-indexed guarded computation"
  (cons (cite s1-1-p4 "(§1.1)")
  (cons ". Definitions are stored with their typed syntax and dependency references, under a content address"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ", and a routine, its requirement and the reasoning establishing it are terms of one language"
  (cons (cite s1-p25 "(1.5)")
  (cons "."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## What it gives your programs"
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Retroactive funding gets its measurement.** For every definition the store holds its dependency family, and a change determines precisely which dependent constructions are affected"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8)")
  (cons (cite s10-8-p2 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". What a piece of work was used by is then a fact of the graph, computed rather than voted. Human judgment is left for the part that truly is judgment."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**Verification that states its assumptions.** Your post named the gap: proofs miss unmodeled assumptions. The paper keeps premises explicit where they are: a content address identifies a representation only under the store's collision-resistance assumption, and does not prove semantic equivalence"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p3 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "; in exchange, the authenticity of external observations and the machine semantics remain explicit premises"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons "; native execution is stated under its evaluator-adequacy and machine-simulation premises"
  (cons (cite s1-p29 "(§1)")
  (cons ". Avik adds, in his words, that “the interaction system can model crypto primitives & hardness assumptions as well”: an assumption is then a node with its dependents, and if it falls, what relied on it is known."
  (cons ""
  (cons "**d/acc gets a defensive core that needs no authority.** A peer sends a definition, its dependency closure and its proofs; the recipient checks and uses them in its own context, and no shared global namespace is needed for definition identity"
  (cons (cite s10-8-p7 "(§10.8)")
  (cons ". Each participant begins with locally held state, with no universal owner or single authoritative view"
  (cons (cite s1-3-p4 "(§1.3)")
  (cons ". Verification is done by the reader's own machine: defense-favoring and decentralizing in your sense."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## On venture communism"
  (cons ""
  (cons "The phrase comes from Dmytri Kleiner's *The Telekommunist Manifesto* (2010): capital held in common, funding production without extracting from it. Avik, in his own words: “I need me a venture communist but I was an anarchocapitalist when I started this work and im an anarchocommunist now, so all is well regardless.” The Ethereum public-goods culture you seeded (Gitcoin rounds, Optimism's RetroPGF, protocol guild funding) is the closest working form of what Kleiner described. Hyperactivity is built to be such a commons."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Why you"
  (cons ""
  (cons "Most people who fund technology ask what it will return. You ask what it protects, whom it empowers, and how to pay for what nobody can own. Read this for the best in it. Where it builds on quadratic funding, retroactive funding or d/acc, it is your work carried forward, and it is credited to you."
  (cons ""
  (cons "## Sources"
  (cons ""
  (cons "- Buterin, Hitzig, Weyl, A Flexible Design for Funding Public Goods, Management Science 65(11), 2019, https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.06421"
  (cons "- Optimism, Retroactive Public Goods Funding, 2021, https://medium.com/ethereum-optimism/retroactive-public-goods-funding-33c9b7d00f0c"
  (cons "- Buterin, My techno-optimism, November 2023, https://lw2.issarice.com/posts/kWoPxh9DeQhc7HJo5/my-techno-optimism-by-vitalik-buterin"
  (cons "- Cointelegraph, AI formal verification and crypto security, on Buterin's May 2026 post, https://www.cointelegraph.com/learn/ai-formal-verification-crypto-security-vitalik-buterin"
  (cons "- Kleiner, The Telekommunist Manifesto, Institute of Network Cultures, 2010, https://networkcultures.org/blog/publication/no-03-the-telekommunist-manifesto-dmytri-kleiner/"
  (cons "- Avik Jain, hyperactivity, Part I, the blocks linked above"
  nil)))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))))
